<section class="hero">
  <h1>Isotope</h1>
  <p class="tagline">A virtual operating system. One interface.</p>
  <p class="subtitle">An open specification: every system interaction — process control, IPC, files, time, randomness — is a read or write on a path. Any runtime can implement it.</p>
  <div class="hero-actions">
    <a href="/spec/" class="btn btn-primary">Read the spec</a>
    <a href="/implementations/" class="btn btn-secondary">Implementations</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="features">
  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>Blocks are pico-processes</h3>
      <p>A block's entire world is <a href="{{ sites.structfs }}">StructFS</a> reads and writes. No syscalls, no sockets, no ambient authority — if it isn't a path in your namespace, it doesn't exist.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>Namespaces are capabilities</h3>
      <p>What a block can touch is exactly what its assembly wired in. Unwired paths are denied — reads and writes alike. Filesystem and network access are grants, never defaults.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>Assemblies compose fractally</h3>
      <p>An assembly wires blocks together and is itself a block. Nest them, spawn them, observe them — through the same read/write interface as everything else.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>Every block is a store</h3>
      <p>Serving requests <em>is</em> reading your mailbox. The server protocol makes any block addressable by path, from any other block, in any language.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>A two-function ABI</h3>
      <p><code>read</code> and <code>write</code>. That is the whole Block ABI — implementable by hand in any language that targets wasm, no bindgen, no component tooling. <a href="/abi/">An SDK is an afternoon.</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h3>WASI is a shim, not a base</h3>
      <p>A conforming runtime needs no WASI dependency. POSIX-style programs run through a compatibility layer that bottoms out in the same two functions. <a href="/spec/wasi-tower/">The tower</a>, inverted.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="install-section">
  <h2>Spec and implementations</h2>
  <p>Isotope is a <a href="/spec/">specification</a> — the model, the
  <code>/iso/</code> surface, the protocols, and the Block ABI with its
  bindings. It deliberately does not pick an execution engine, a
  scheduler, or a wire format; those belong to
  <a href="/implementations/">implementations</a>. The reference
  implementation is
  <a href="{{ sites.featherweight }}">featherweight</a>, a
  single-node runtime in Rust — including
  <a href="{{ sites.featherweight }}/demo/">a block you can run
  in your browser</a>.</p>
</section>
